WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.960
An AI agent is more than a chatbot that answers questions.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:18.420
In practical terms, an agent is a software system that can use an AI model to interpret goals, plan steps, use tools, access data, remember context, and sometimes take actions on behalf of a user or organization.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:26.720
The agent may read documents, call APIs, send messages, update tickets, write code, query databases, or coordinate with other agents.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:33.340
Those capabilities can make agentic systems useful in real workflows, but they also expand the attack surface.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.160
The key difference is action.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:39.300
A simple chatbot usually produces text for a person to evaluate.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.680
An agent may connect model output to tools, memory, permissions, data, and business processes.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.540
When a model can act, mistakes and manipulation can have consequences beyond the chat window.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.840
Secure agent design starts by treating the agent as software, not magic.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:07.460
The important components are the model, the application code, tool integrations, identity and authorization, data sources, memory, workflow rules, logging, monitoring, and human approval paths.
